issues.body:
Drafted with AI assistance from a review pass; quotes and citations verified against the source documents.

-03 Appendix A defines the structure with two identity fields:

```
   RPCAuthSys ::= SEQUENCE {
       uid        INTEGER (0..4294967295),  -- 32-bit UID
       gids       SEQUENCE OF INTEGER (0..4294967295)  -- List of 32-bit GIDs
   }
```

§3.4.2 describes it the same way, and refers the meaning of the integers to RFC 5531:

```
   The otherName value for AUTH_SYS identities contains an RPCAuthSys
   structure as defined in Appendix A.  This structure consists of a
   32-bit unsigned integer specifying a numeric UID, and a sequence of
   32-bit unsigned integers specifying numeric GIDs.
```

RFC 5531 Appendix A has three identity fields, and distinguishes the effective group ID from the supplementary list:

```
      struct authsys_parms {
         unsigned int stamp;
         string machinename<255>;
         unsigned int uid;
         unsigned int gid;
         unsigned int gids<16>;
      };
```

```
   The "uid" is the caller's effective user ID.  The "gid"
   is the caller's effective group ID.  "gids" are a counted array of
   groups that contain the caller as a member.
```

A server building an AUTH_SYS credential from an RPCAuthSys value has no field for the effective GID. One implementation takes gids[0] as the effective GID and the rest as supplementary; another treats the whole list as supplementary and supplies an effective GID from its own user database. The two authorize differently against group-owned files from the same certificate. Appendix B.6.2 Test Case 2 reaches the case where neither reading works: uid 500 with an empty GID list yields no effective GID at all.

The 16-element bound is also absent, since SEQUENCE OF INTEGER carries no SIZE constraint. A certificate carrying 40 GIDs is well-formed under -03 and cannot be represented in the credential RFC 5531 defines. The bound was in the text and was removed in caeecf3d18c8, the -00 commit, which replaced "up to 16 numeric GIDs" with "a counted list of 32-bit integers that specify numeric GIDs"; that commit is otherwise about adding the Kerberos identity form and records no reason for the change.

Add an effective-GID field, or say which element of gids is the effective GID. Say whether the 16-element AUTH_SYS limit applies, and what a server does with a longer list.
